Grothendieck conjectured (1960s) that all cohomology theories (Betti,
de Rham, l-adic, crystalline) are representations of a single underlying
algebraic structure — the **motive**. The motive is the "DNA" of the
geometry: it knows the volume, the Betti numbers, the Hodge diamond,
and the L-function, all as different projections of the same object.

Okounkov (Fields Medal 2006) proved that Gromov-Witten invariants —
the integer counts of holomorphic curves in a target space — are
governed by Random Matrix Theory. The partition function of GW theory
IS the tau-function of a random matrix ensemble.

This file connects these two 20th-century masterworks through the
chiral algebra M₂(ℂ) = span{N₊, N₋, S₊, S₋}, whose 5-graded TKK
closure provides the explicit motive-theoretic structure.

